answer:
Below is a complete, **v6-only** example that

1. registers the **PMTiles** protocol,
2. adds a vector-tile source that reads from a local `.pmtiles` file, and
3. draws a simple line layer from one of the source’s layers.

You can copy-paste the HTML (or adapt the JavaScript for a bundler) and it will work with **MapLibre GL JS v6** and **@protomaps/pmtiles**.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the two packages

```bash
# npm / yarn / pnpm
npm i maplibre-gl @protomaps/pmtiles
```

> **Why these two?**  
> *`maplibre-gl`* provides the map engine.  
> *`@protomaps/pmtiles`* implements the `pmtiles://` URL protocol that lets MapLibre read a `.pmtiles` archive as a vector-tile source.

---

## 2️⃣ Minimal HTML + JS (CDN-only, no bundler)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>PMTiles → MapLibre line layer</title>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>

  <!-- 1️⃣ MapLibre v6 ESM build -->
  <script type="module">
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // Imports (v6 uses named/namespace imports – no default export)
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';
    import { PMTiles } from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@protomaps/pmtiles@latest/dist/index.min.js';

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2️⃣ Register the pmtiles:// protocol
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // The URL you pass to the protocol can be any string you like – it
    // just has to be unique for each archive you want to expose.
    const pmtilesUrl = new URL('my-data.pmtiles', import.meta.url).toString(); // <-- change this path
    const pm = new PMTiles(pmtilesUrl);
    // `pmtiles.Protocol` implements the fetch-handler expected by MapLibre.
    const protocol = new pm.Protocol();

    // Tell MapLibre to use the handler for the "pmtiles" scheme.
    maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣ Create the map
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    const map = new maplibregl.Map({
      container: 'map',
      style: {
        version: 8,
        sources: {},          // we’ll add our source later
        layers: []            // we’ll add our layer later
      },
      center: [0, 0],         // arbitrary start position
      zoom: 2
    });

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣ When the map is ready, add the vector source & line layer
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    map.on('load', () => {
      // ---- 4a️⃣ Vector source that reads from the .pmtiles file ----
      map.addSource('my-pmtiles-source', {
        type: 'vector',
        // The URL uses the custom scheme we just registered.
        // The part after "pmtiles://" is the identifier we gave the archive.
        url: 'pmtiles://my-data.pmtiles'
      });

      // ---- 4b️⃣ Line layer ------------------------------------------------
      // You need to know the name of the layer inside the vector tiles.
      // You can discover it with `map.getStyle().sources['my-pmtiles-source']....`
      // or by inspecting the archive with `pmtiles info my-data.pmtiles`.
      const sourceLayerName = 'roads';   // <-- replace with your actual layer name

      map.addLayer({
        id: 'my-line-layer',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'my-pmtiles-source',
        'source-layer': sourceLayerName,
        layout: {
          // optional: smooth line joins / caps
          'line-join': 'round',
          'line-cap': 'round'
        },
        paint: {
          'line-color': '#ff6600',   // bright orange
          'line-width': 3,
          'line-opacity': 0.9
        }
      });
    });
  </script>
</head>

<body>
  <div id="map"></div>
</body>
</html>
```

### What you need to change

| Placeholder | What to replace | Why |
|-------------|----------------|-----|
| `my-data.pmtiles` (two occurrences) | Path/URL to **your** `.pmtiles` file (relative to the page or an absolute URL) | The PMTiles library loads the archive from this location. |
| `roads` (the `sourceLayerName`) | The exact name of the vector-tile layer that contains the line geometry you want to draw | You can discover it with `pmtiles info my-data.pmtiles` or by opening the archive in a viewer. |
| `center` / `zoom` | Any initial view you prefer | Optional – the map will automatically pan/zoom to the data once you add a `fitBounds` call if you like. |

---

## 3️⃣ If you’re using a bundler (Vite, webpack, Rollup, …)

The same code works, you just import from the installed packages instead of the CDN:

```js
// src/main.js
import { Map, addProtocol } from 'maplibre-gl';
import { PMTiles } from '@protomaps/pmtiles';

// Register protocol (same as above)
const pm = new PMTiles(new URL('my-data.pmtiles', import.meta.url).toString());
addProtocol('pmtiles', new pm.Protocol().tile);

// …create the map and add source/layer exactly as shown earlier…
```

**Bundler-only note:** v6 still requires a one-time `setWorkerUrl()` call when the code is bundled (see the migration guide). If you hit a “worker failed to load” error, add:

```js
import { setWorkerUrl } from 'maplibre-gl';
import workerUrl from 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl-worker.mjs?worker&url';
setWorkerUrl(workerUrl);
```

(For a plain CDN `<script type="module">` you **do not** need this call.)

---

## 4️⃣ Quick sanity-check checklist

- ✅ **v6 import style** – `import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl'` or named imports. No default export.
- ✅ **PMTiles protocol** – `maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile)`.
- ✅ **Vector source** – `type: 'vector'`, `url: 'pmtiles://<identifier>'`.
- ✅ **Line layer** – `type: 'line'`, correct `source-layer` name, paint properties.
- ✅ **Worker URL** – only needed for bundlers, not for CDN ESM.

That’s it! Your map will now read vector tiles directly from the `.pmtiles` archive and render the requested line features. Happy mapping!

