answer:
Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste-ready tutorial** that shows how to:

1. Load a **PMTiles** file (a single-file container for vector tiles) in the browser.  
2. Register the `pmtiles://` protocol that MapLibre GL JS understands.  
3. Add the PMTiles file as a **vector source**.  
4. Create a **styled line layer** that draws the features from that source.

You can run the example locally (or on any static web host) with just an HTML file and the two CDN scripts shown.

---

## 1️⃣  What you need

| Item | Why? | CDN / npm |
|------|------|-----------|
| **MapLibre GL JS** | The map engine (open-source fork of Mapbox GL JS) | `https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js` |
| **PMTiles JS library** | Reads the `.pmtiles` file and serves tile data via a custom protocol | `https://unpkg.com/pmtiles@2.7.0/dist/index.js` |
| **A PMTiles file** | The vector-tile data you want to display (e.g. `roads.pmtiles`) | Put it next to your HTML or host it somewhere reachable |

> **Tip:** If you are using a bundler (Webpack, Vite, etc.) you can `npm i maplibre-gl pmtiles` and import them instead of the CDN links. The code below works unchanged with the CDN version.

---

## 2️⃣  Minimal HTML skeleton

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre + PMTiles – line layer demo</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">

  <!-- MapLibre GL CSS (required for the default UI) -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />

  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>

<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<!-- 1️⃣ MapLibre GL JS -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

<!-- 2️⃣ PMTiles library -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/pmtiles@2.7.0/dist/index.js"></script>

<!-- 3️⃣ Your map-initialisation script (see next section) -->
<script>
  // ----  👉  EVERYTHING you need is inside this <script> block  👈 ----
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Save the file as `index.html` (or any name you like) and place your `roads.pmtiles` file in the same folder.

---

## 3️⃣  JavaScript – wiring everything together

Paste the following **inside the `<script>` tag** that you see at the bottom of the HTML skeleton.

```js
/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   1️⃣  Register the pmtiles:// protocol
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const { PMTiles } = window.PMTiles;   // the library puts itself on window

// 👉  Replace "roads.pmtiles" with the name/path of your own file.
const pmtilesUrl = "roads.pmtiles";

// Create a PMTiles object that knows how to fetch the file.
const pmtiles = new PMTiles(pmtilesUrl);

// Tell MapLibre how to resolve a request that starts with "pmtiles://"
maplibregl.addProtocol("pmtiles", (params, abort) => {
  // `params` contains { url, ... } where url is like "pmtiles://roads.pmtiles/12/345/678.pbf"
  // The PMTiles instance can serve any tile request from the same file.
  return pmtiles.get(params, abort);
});

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   2️⃣  Initialise the map
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: "map",                     // id of the div above
  style: "https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json", // any base style you like
  center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],     // lon, lat – adjust to your data
  zoom: 12,
  hash: true
});

// Add navigation UI (optional but handy)
map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl());

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   3️⃣  Add the PMTiles vector source once the map is ready
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
map.on("load", () => {
  // ----  a)  Register the source  ----
  map.addSource("roads-pmtiles", {
    type: "vector",
    // The URL uses the custom protocol we just registered.
    // The part after the file name ("/") is optional – we just give the file.
    url: "pmtiles://" + pmtilesUrl
  });

  // ----  b)  Find out the source-layer name(s) inside the PMTiles file
  //    (You can also look at the .pmtiles metadata with a tool like tippecanoe,
  //     but here we query the map after the source is loaded.)
  const source = map.getSource("roads-pmtiles");
  // `source.vectorLayerIds` is an array of layer names that exist in the tiles.
  // For a simple demo we just pick the first one.
  const layerName = source.vectorLayerIds[0]; // e.g. "road"

  console.log("PMTiles source-layer names:", source.vectorLayerIds);
  console.log("Using source-layer:", layerName);

  // ----  c)  Add a line layer that draws from that source-layer  ----
  map.addLayer({
    id: "roads-line",
    type: "line",
    source: "roads-pmtiles",
    "source-layer": layerName,   // <-- the name we just discovered
    layout: {
      // Optional: make lines follow the direction of the road (if you have a "oneway" property)
      "line-cap": "round",
      "line-join": "round"
    },
    paint: {
      // Basic styling – feel free to tweak!
      "line-color": [
        // Example: colour by a property called "class"
        "match",
        ["get", "class"],
        "motorway", "#ff6600",
        "primary", "#ffcc00",
        "secondary", "#ffff00",
        "tertiary", "#99ff33",
        /* default */ "#66ccff"
      ],
      "line-width": [
        // Width varies with zoom level (makes it look crisp)
        "interpolate",
        ["linear"],
        ["zoom"],
        10, 1,
        14, 3,
        18, 8
      ],
      "line-opacity": 0.9
    }
  });
});
```

### What the script does, step-by-step

| Step | Code | Explanation |
|------|------|-------------|
| **Register protocol** | `maplibregl.addProtocol("pmtiles", …)` | MapLibre only knows `http://`, `https://`, `mapbox://`, etc. By adding a custom protocol we tell it how to fetch a tile from a PMTiles file. |
| **Create `PMTiles` object** | `new PMTiles(pmtilesUrl)` | The library reads the file (via `fetch`) and can serve any tile request (`z/x/y.pbf`) from it. |
| **Initialize map** | `new maplibregl.Map({ … })` | Standard MapLibre setup – you can replace the base style with any style you like (e.g. a light or dark theme). |
| **Add vector source** | `map.addSource("roads-pmtiles", {type:"vector", url:"pmtiles://roads.pmtiles"})` | The source points to the PMTiles file via the custom protocol. |
| **Discover source-layer name** | `source.vectorLayerIds` | PMTiles can contain many vector layers (e.g. `road`, `building`, `water`). The array tells you the exact names you must use in `"source-layer"`. |
| **Add line layer** | `map.addLayer({ … })` | The actual visual layer. The `paint` block shows a few common styling tricks (color by property, width that scales with zoom). |

---

## 4️⃣  Styling tips & common gotchas

| Issue | Fix / Tip |
|-------|-----------|
| **No line appears** | 1️⃣ Open the browser console – you should see the list of `source-layer` names. <br>2️⃣ Make sure you use the exact name in `"source-layer"` (case-sensitive). <br>3️⃣ Verify that the source actually contains line geometries (e.g. `LineString` or `MultiLineString`). |
| **Tiles are not loading (404 / CORS)** | The PMTiles file must be reachable via a normal `fetch`. If you host it on a different domain, ensure that the server sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain). |
| **Performance feels sluggish** | PMTiles is already a single-file, indexed container, but you can improve speed by: <ul><li>Serving the file with `Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000`.</li><li>Using a CDN that supports range requests (the library needs byte-range reads).</li></ul> |
| **Want to style by zoom-dependent colour** | Use an `interpolate` expression in `"line-color"` just like the `"line-width"` example. |
| **Multiple layers in the same PMTiles** | Add more `map.addLayer({ … })` calls, each with a different `"source-layer"` value. |
| **Using a remote PMTiles URL** | Replace `pmtilesUrl = "roads.pmtiles"` with the full URL (e.g. `"https://my.cdn.com/tiles/roads.pmtiles"`). The same protocol registration works. |

